Here's a possible recursive binary search implementation:

```
public static int binarySearch(int[] arr, int x, int left, int right) {
    if (left > right) { // base case: element not found
        return -1;
    }

    int middle = left + (right - left) / 2; // find the middle index

    if (arr[middle] == x) { // base case: element found
        return middle;
    } else if (arr[middle] < x) { // search in the right half of the array
        return binarySearch(arr, x, middle + 1, right);
    } else { // search in the left half of the array
        return binarySearch(arr, x, left, middle - 1);
    }
}

// example usage:
int[] arr = new int[]{2, 5, 8, 12, 16, 23, 38, 56, 72, 91};
int x = 23;
int index = binarySearch(arr, x, 0, arr.length - 1);
System.out.println(index); // 5
```

The `binarySearch` method takes in the array to search `arr`, the element to search for `x`, and the indices `left` and `right` that define the current search range. Initially, `left` is `0` and `right` is `arr.length - 1`.

First, the method tests if the current search range is empty (`left > right`). If so, it means that the element is not in the array, so `-1` is returned (base case).

Otherwise, the method finds the middle index of the current range (`middle`), using the formula `middle = left + (right - left) / 2` (which avoids potential integer overflow when `left` and `right` are large). If the element at `arr[middle]` is equal to the search element `x`, then `middle` is returned (base case).

If the element at `arr[middle]` is less than `x`, it means that the search element can only be in the right half of the array (because the array is sorted in ascending order). So the method is called recursively with `left = middle + 1` and `right` unchanged.

Otherwise, if the element at `arr[middle]` is greater than `x`, it means that the search element can only be in the left half of the array. So the method is called recursively with `left` unchanged and `right = middle - 1`.

By doing this, the method progressively narrows down the search range until the element is found or the range becomes empty, in which case `-1` is returned. Since the search range is halved at every recursive call, the algorithm has a logarithmic time complexity, i.e., O(log n).

