// assume that searchString is the title that the user is searching for
String searchString = ...;

// binary search for the song title
int left = 0;
int right = userSavedSongs.length - 1;
int resultIndex = -1; // initialize to -1 (not found)

while (left <= right) {
    int middle = left + (right - left) / 2;
    String currentTitle = userSavedSongs[middle].getTitle();
    int cmp = currentTitle.compareTo(searchString);

    if (cmp == 0) { // song title found
        resultIndex = middle;
        break;
    } else if (cmp < 0) { // search in the right half
        left = middle + 1;
    } else { // search in the left half
        right = middle - 1;
    }
}

if (resultIndex >= 0) {
    // found the song at index resultIndex
    Song foundSong = userSavedSongs[resultIndex];
    // display song details or do something else
} else {
    // song not found
    System.out.println("Song not found.");
}
```

In this example, the binary search algorithm takes the user's saved songs array `userSavedSongs`, the title to search for `searchString`, and iteratively narrows down the search range until the desired song is found. If the song title is not found, the algorithm returns -1 which can then be used to notify the user that the song was not found.

By using binary search, you're able to efficiently search through a large array in O(log n) time and provide a responsive user experience to users of your app.

