```js
function validateEmail(email) {
  const emailRegex = /^\w+([\.-]?\w+)*@\w+([\.-]?\w+)*(\.\w{2,6})+$/;
  // ^            Beginning of string
  // \w+          One or more word characters (a-z, A-Z, 0-9, _)
  // ([\.-]?\w+)* A group that can occur zero or more times (optional period or hyphen followed by one or more word characters)
  // @            Literal @ symbol
  // \w+          One or more word characters (a-z, A-Z, 0-9, _)
  // ([\.-]?\w+)* A group that can occur zero or more times (optional period or hyphen followed by one or more word characters)
  // (\.\w{2,6})+ One or more groups of a period followed by two to six word characters (the extension)
  // $            End of string
  const isValidEmail = emailRegex.test(email);
  return isValidEmail;
}
```

The regular expression `/^\w+([\.-]?\w+)*@\w+([\.-]?\w+)*(\.\w{2,6})+$/` has the following parts:

- `^`: Beginning of string
- `\w+`: Match one or more word characters (a-z, A-Z, 0-9, _)
- `([\.-]?\w+)*`: Match zero or more groups that start with an optional period or hyphen, followed by one or more word characters.
- `@`: Literal @ symbol
- `\w+`: Match one or more word characters (a-z, A-Z, 0-9, _)
- `([\.-]?\w+)*`: Match zero or more groups that start with an optional period or hyphen, followed by one or more word characters.
- `(\.\w{2,6})+`: Match one or more groups that start with a period, followed by 2-6 word characters (this is the domain extension)
- `$`: End of string.

The `test()` method returns `true` if the passed string matches the regular expression, and `false` otherwise.

